ArgoCD Architecture

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m exploring the architecture of Argo CD 🚀
Understanding its components helps in knowing how GitOps automation works internally.

🔹 API Server
 The API Server acts as the central component of ArgoCD.
Handles user requests (UI/CLI)
Manages application definitions
Communicates with other components
It is the entry point for interacting with ArgoCD.

🔹 Repo Server
 The Repo Server is responsible for:
Fetching configurations from Git repositories
Reading Kubernetes YAML manifests
Preparing deployment data
It ensures the system always uses the latest configuration from Git.

🔹 Application Controller
 This is the core component of ArgoCD.
Compares desired state (Git) with actual state (cluster)
Detects configuration drift
Applies changes to match the desired state
It enables continuous reconciliation and self-healing.

🔹 UI (User Interface)
 ArgoCD provides a web-based UI to:
Visualize applications
Monitor deployment status
Trigger manual sync if needed
It makes GitOps workflows easy to understand and manage visually.

💡 Key Insight:
 ArgoCD architecture works together to ensure that Kubernetes clusters always stay in sync with Git repositories automatically.
